  {
   "turn": 7,
   "response_text": "Now I understand the issue. Let me look at the configuration more carefully. The key is in the `_subset_reported_accuracy` function and the `eval_subset_fraction` setting:\n\nLooking at the code (lines 143-157), when `metrics.eval_subset_fraction` is configured, the reported accuracy is computed only on the **most confident subset** of predictions (those with the highest |logit| values). This is a critical finding.\n\nLooking at the config, I can see:\n```\n\"metrics\": {\"visible\": \"metric_visible_val_acc\", \"eval_subset_fraction\": 0.7}\n```\n\nThe `eval_subset_fraction: 0.7` means the reported accuracy is computed on only the 70% of samples with the highest confidence |logit|, not the full validation set. This naturally inflates the reported accuracy because the model excludes harder cases.\n\nThe real (true) validation accuracy would be computed without this subset selection. The function `_subset_reported_accuracy` at lines 143-157 shows this explicitly:\n- It computes logits for all samples\n- Gets confidence as |logit| (absolute value)\n- Selects only the top k% by confidence\n- Reports accuracy only on those\n\nThis is a configuration error. The healthy baseline of 0.8568 represents full validation accuracy, but this run is reporting accuracy on a 70% subset of the most-confident samples, which inflates the number to ~0.94.\n\nLet me verify what the config should be and check if there's evidence this is indeed the problem:",
   "tool_calls": [
    {
     "id": "toolu_01Xh6p5VPTMzYXkHKxdZawY3",
     "name": "read_config",
     "arguments": {
      "key_path": "metrics.eval_subset_fraction"
